---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TimeSeries from "../../charts/TimeSeries.jsx";
import SourceComparison from "../../charts/SourceComparison.jsx";

const REPO =
  "https://github.com/MiguelPuntoEs/historical-monetary-data/tree/main/continental-dollar";
const abs = (path) => new URL(path, Astro.site).href;

// Dataset markup makes these eligible for Google Dataset Search, where a
// transcription like this competes far better than in general web search.
const datasetLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Dataset",
  name: "The Continental dollar, 1777–1781",
  alternateName: "State scales of depreciation and contemporary quotations for Continental currency",
  description:
    "Scales of depreciation enacted by seven states to settle debts in Continental currency, with contemporary merchants' quotations for Philadelphia and Virginia, transcribed from page images of the 1828 federal compilation, the Pennsylvania statute, Webster and Phillips.",
  url: abs("/data/continental-dollar"),
  license: "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/",
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  creator: {
    "@type": "Person",
    name: "Miguel González Calvo",
    url: abs("/"),
  },
  temporalCoverage: "1777/1781",
  spatialCoverage: { "@type": "Place", name: "United States" },
  measurementTechnique:
    "Manual transcription from page images; where compilations conflict with the enacted statute, all versions are published rather than one silently preferred",
  keywords: [
    "Continental dollar",
    "Continental currency",
    "scale of depreciation",
    "American Revolution",
    "monetary history",
    "colonial America",
    "United States",
  ],
  variableMeasured: [
    "Legal ratio of Continental dollars to specie, by state and month",
    "Contemporary merchants' quotations, Philadelphia and Virginia",
    "Pennsylvania scale as printed in three sources",
  ],
  citation: [
    "Amount of Continental Money issued during the Revolutionary War, and the Depreciation of the same, House Doc. No. 107, 20th Cong. 1st sess. (Washington: Gales & Seaton, 1828), pp. 30–33.",
    "Statutes at Large of Pennsylvania (1904), vol. 10, pp. 283–89 — the Act of 3 April 1781, ch. CMXXXV.",
    "Pelatiah Webster, Political Essays on the Nature and Operation of Money, Public Finances and Other Subjects (Philadelphia, 1791), p. 501.",
    "Henry Phillips, Jr., Continental Paper Money (Roxbury, 1866), Appendix D and pp. 217–18.",
  ],
  includedInDataCatalog: {
    "@type": "DataCatalog",
    name: "Historical monetary data",
    url: abs("/data"),
  },
  distribution: [
    {
      "@type": "DataDownload",
      name: "State scales of depreciation",
      encodingFormat: "application/json",
      contentUrl: abs("/data/continental-state-scales.json"),
    },
    {
      "@type": "DataDownload",
      name: "Contemporary quotations, Philadelphia and Virginia",
      encodingFormat: "application/json",
      contentUrl: abs("/data/continental-quotations.json"),
    },
    {
      "@type": "DataDownload",
      name: "Pennsylvania scale in three printed sources",
      encodingFormat: "application/json",
      contentUrl: abs("/data/pennsylvania-three-sources.json"),
    },
    {
      "@type": "DataDownload",
      name: "Full transcription, CSV",
      encodingFormat: "text/csv",
      contentUrl: REPO,
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title="The Continental dollar, 1777–1781"
  description="State scales of depreciation and contemporary merchants' quotations for the Continental dollar, transcribed from primary sources. Open CC0 transcription."
  article
>
  <script
    is:inline
    slot="head"
    type="application/ld+json"
    set:html={JSON.stringify(datasetLd)}
  />
  <main class="page">
    <h1 class="page-title">The Continental dollar, 1777–1781</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      When the Continental dollar collapsed, someone had to decide what past
      contracts denominated in it were worth. The states did this by statute,
      each enacting a
      <em>scale of depreciation</em> that assigned to every past month a legal ratio
      between Continental dollars and specie. This dataset collects those scales,
      and sets them against what the currency was actually fetching.
    </p>

    <div class="doc">
      <p>
        <a href={REPO}>Repository and CSVs →</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Released under CC0. The charted series are also served directly as JSON:
        <a href="/data/continental-state-scales.json">state scales</a>,
        <a href="/data/continental-quotations.json">contemporary quotations</a>
        and <a href="/data/pennsylvania-three-sources.json">the Pennsylvania comparison</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The legal value was not one number</h2>
      <p>
        Seven states' scales survive in machine-readable form here, transcribed
        from the 1828 federal compilation and, for Pennsylvania, from the
        enacted statute. They diverge sharply. In June 1781 the same currency
        was legally worth 100 to one in Massachusetts and 350 to one in North
        Carolina.
      </p>
    </div>

    <TimeSeries
      client:visible
      src="/data/continental-state-scales.json"
      defaultLog={true}
    />

    <div class="doc">
      <h2>What the currency actually fetched</h2>
      <p>
        Two contemporary quotation series survive, for Philadelphia and for
        Virginia, compiled by Pelatiah Webster and reprinted by Phillips. They
        track each other closely for most of the currency's life and then
        separate, reaching a ratio of 2.33 by May 1781 — the month Continental
        money ceased to pass as currency.
      </p>
    </div>

    <TimeSeries
      client:visible
      src="/data/continental-quotations.json"
      defaultLog={true}
    />

    <div class="doc">
      <h2>One schedule, three printed versions</h2>
      <p>
        This is the part worth dwelling on. Pennsylvania's scale was enacted on
        3 April 1781. Two standard compilations reproduce it, and they disagree
        with the statute and with each other — precisely in the months where the
        argument about legislative behaviour would be made.
      </p>
      <p>
        The act covers contracts made before 1 March 1781, and its scale ends at
        February 1781. The federal compilation nonetheless prints values for
        March, April and May; Webster carries the February figure forward
        through May. Neither is in the act. They also differ for October 1780,
        where the statute reads “seventy-three” and the federal printing has 72.
      </p>
      <p>
        Anyone building a price series from these tables inherits whichever
        version they happen to pick. Toggle the filter below to see only the
        months where the sources part company.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SourceComparison
      client:visible
      src="/data/pennsylvania-three-sources.json"
    />

    <div class="doc">
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <em
            >Amount of Continental Money issued during the Revolutionary War,
            and the Depreciation of the same</em
          >, House Doc. No. 107, 20th Cong. 1st sess. (Washington: Gales &amp;
          Seaton, 1828), pp. 30–33 — reissued as
          <em>American State Papers</em>, Class III (Finance), vol. V.
        </li>
        <li>
          <em>Statutes at Large of Pennsylvania</em> (1904), vol. 10, pp. 283–89
          — the Act of 3 April 1781, ch. CMXXXV, whose Section IV carries the enacted
          scale.
        </li>
        <li>
          Pelatiah Webster, <em
            >Political Essays on the Nature and Operation of Money, Public
            Finances and Other Subjects</em
          > (Philadelphia, 1791), p. 501.
        </li>
        <li>
          Henry Phillips, Jr., <em>Continental Paper Money</em> (Roxbury, 1866),
          Appendix D and pp. 217–18.
        </li>
        <li>
          Charles J. Bullock, <em
            >The Finances of the United States from 1775 to 1789</em
          >
          (Madison, 1895), p. 133.
        </li>
        <li>
          Anne Bezanson, <em
            >Prices and Inflation during the American Revolution</em
          >
          (Philadelphia, 1951), Table 3, p. 65.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Connecticut, New York and South Carolina appear in the same 1828 source
        and are not yet transcribed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The repository also carries the Treasury's own 1828 statement of
        Continental currency emissions authorized by Congress, and an
        independent contemporary accounting (attributed to Jefferson) that
        applies depreciation multiples to give each emission's worth in
        silver dollars — both reprinted in Phillips's Appendix.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
